      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The aim of this prospective cohort study was to determine the densitometric relevance of minor design modifications of a cementless stem designed to improve proximal load transfer. We used a prospective cohort study with densitometric analysis over a five-year period of two groups of patients with primary osteoarthritis. The first group, 56 hips, received the first version of the ABG stem (ABG-I); the second group, 54 hips, had the ABG-II stem. The results obtained with the ABG-I stem showed a decrease of bone density in proximal areas that ranged from 13% to 37%. However, the new design had a decrease of the same areas that ranged from 9% to 23%. These differences were noted at the end of the first post-operative year and remained stable, except in zone 7, where they were progressive. There is little evidence that the modified stem reduces femoral bone density loss."},
